SECTION 5 — INITIALIZATION, HALT, AND FAILURE RISKS

Review startup and execution order for primary entry points.

Verify that:

Validation occurs before destructive processing.
Configuration is initialized before use.
Worksheets and templates are resolved before access.
Locks are acquired before protected operations.
Locks are released on success and failure.
Caches are initialized before use.
Required values are assigned on every branch.
Early returns do not leave partial processing.
Errors are not swallowed while processing continues incorrectly.
Success notifications occur only after completion.
Timing start and completion calls are balanced.
Cleanup occurs after dependent work is finished.

Identify realistic halt conditions such as:

Syntax or reference errors
Null or undefined access
Invalid sheet or range access
Missing templates
Missing headers
Invalid indexes
Missing returns
Unexpected return types
Uncaught exceptions
Permission failures
Lock failures
Quota or time-limit risks
Partial writes
Cleanup failures

Use one consolidated register:

ID	Severity	Entry Point	Function/Module	Halt or Corruption Risk	Evidence	Required Correction

Severity:

Critical: Project cannot load or an entry point cannot start.
High: Workflow will stop, corrupt data, or perform an invalid destructive action.
Medium: Workflow may fail under a realistic condition.
Low: Nonblocking issue that increases future failure risk.

List all Critical and High findings.

Limit Medium and Low findings to the most important items needed to understand readiness.

When multiple findings have the same root cause, consolidate them into one finding and list the affected components.

